[sv-migrate]: https://svelte.dev/docs/cli/sv-migrate
# Migrating from mdsvex 0.x

Layouts are now [templates](/docs/guides/templates). The idea is the same, a component that wraps documents and replaces their elements, with new names and Svelte 5 components.

::marks

## Run the migrator

`@mdsvex/migrate` rewrites your config and documents, and flags what it can't rewrite:

```bash
npm i --save-dev @mdsvex/migrate
```

It is a library of functions. A short script runs them over a project. Commit your work first, then set `config` to the file that calls `mdsvex()`, `layouts` to your layout components, and `options` to what your documents were written for:

```js title="migrate.js"
import { readFileSync, writeFileSync, globSync } from 'node:fs';
import { migrate_document, migrate_config, check_template } from '@mdsvex/migrate';

const config = 'svelte.config.js';
const layouts = ['src/lib/layouts/Blog.svelte'];
// smartypants: your old smartypants option, true if you never set it
// math: true if you used remark-math
const options = { smartypants: true, math: false };

function report(file, notes) {
	for (const note of notes) console.log(`${file}:${note.line}: ${note.message}`);
}

const { code, notes } = migrate_config(readFileSync(config, 'utf8'));
writeFileSync(config, code);
report(config, notes);

for (const file of globSync('src/**/*.{svx,md}')) {
	const { code, notes } = migrate_document(readFileSync(file, 'utf8'), options);
	writeFileSync(file, code);
	report(file, notes);
}

for (const file of layouts) report(file, check_template(readFileSync(file, 'utf8')));
```

```bash
node migrate.js
```

- `migrate_config` rewrites the options of a JavaScript or TypeScript config: `layout` into `templates`, `highlight`, `smartypants` and `extension`. It returns the new `code` and a list of `notes`, things to check by hand, each with a `line` and a `message`. Every entry of `remarkPlugins` and `rehypePlugins` gets a note that says [what replaces it](/docs/guides/remark-and-rehype).
- `migrate_document` converts a document to mdsvex's [markdown](/docs/learn/markdown) and returns its `code` and `notes`, `migrate` the code alone. 0.x read CommonMark, where `*text*` is emphasis and a `===` underline makes a heading, so every document needs it. Svelte in the document is kept as written, the `layout:` key of the frontmatter becomes `template:`, and GitHub alerts become [callouts](/docs/plugins/callouts).
- `check_template` lists what a layout component needs changed. It rewrites nothing.

Read the diff before you commit it. `migrate` writes each document out again from what it parsed, so list markers, table padding and blank lines come out in one style.

The options stay where they were. SvelteKit 3 has no `svelte.config.js`, so move the `mdsvex()` call from `preprocess` into the `plugins` of `vite.config.js`, as in [SvelteKit](/docs/learn/quick-start#sveltekit). mdsvex registers its extensions, so they don't need to move with it.

For a document that is already mdsvex's markdown, `migrate_frontmatter` renames the `layout:` key and changes nothing else.

## What changes

::::added
:::add[`layout: './src/Layout.svelte'`](mark="layout", tone=other)
`templates: { default: './src/Layout.svelte' }`
:::

:::add[`layout: { _: …, blog: … }`](mark="_:", tone=other)
`templates: { default: …, blog: … }`
:::

:::add[a named layout applied to documents in a folder of the same name](mark="/blog/", tone=other)
`select_template`, which the migrator writes for you as a note
:::

:::add[frontmatter `layout: blog`, `layout: false`](mark="---", tone=text)
`template: blog`, `template: false`
:::

:::add[frontmatter `layout: _`](mark="_", tone=text)
`template: default`
:::

:::add[`layoutPropForwarding`](mark="...", tone=expr)
always on, the migrator flags it
:::

:::add[`<slot />` in a layout](mark="<slot>", tone=comp)
`{@render children()}`
:::

:::add[`export let title` in a layout](mark="export", tone=expr)
`let { title } = $props()`
:::

:::add[`<script context="module">` exports](mark="module", tone=expr)
`<script module>` exports
:::

:::add[`{title}` in a document](mark="{}", tone=expr)
`{metadata.title}`
:::

:::add[`extension: '.md'`](mark=".md", tone=other)
`extensions: ['.md']`
:::

:::add[`frontmatter: { marker, type }`](mark="marker", tone=text)
gone, the fences are always `---` and [`frontmatter.parse`](/docs/guides/frontmatter#custom-frontmatter-parsers) reads what is between them
:::

:::add[`rel="nofollow"` on every link to another site](mark="rel=", tone=link)
no `rel`. `external_links({ rel: ['nofollow'] })` from [`@mdsvex/plugin-external-links`](/docs/plugins/external-links) keeps it
:::

:::add[`smartypants`, on by default](mark="--", tone=text)
[`smart_punctuation`](/docs/reference/options#smart_punctuation), off by default, the migrator turns it on
:::

:::add[`remarkPlugins`, `rehypePlugins`](mark="remark", tone=other)
gone. Most of what they did is built in or a first-party plugin, see [coming from remark and rehype plugins](/docs/guides/remark-and-rehype)
:::

:::add[CommonMark documents](mark="*a*", tone=emph)
mdsvex's own [markdown](/docs/learn/markdown), the migrator converts them
:::

:::add[```` ```js file=./a.js#L1-L5 ````, from `remark-code-import`](mark="file=", tone=fence)
[`src=`](/docs/reference/highlighting#code-from-a-file), the migrator renames `file=` on a fence with no code
:::

:::add[`$x$` and `$$x$$`, from `remark-math`](mark="$x$", tone=code)
[math](/docs/reference/math), the migrator rewrites them when you pass `math: true`
:::

:::add[`![alt](./a.png)` served from the page's url](mark="![]", tone=link)
imported from beside the document, write `/a.png` for a file in `static`, or set [`import_images: false`](/docs/reference/images)
:::
::::

The migrator handles the `layout`, `smartypants` and `extension` entries and converts the documents. It flags `<slot />`, `layoutPropForwarding`, the `frontmatter` options, the missing `nofollow` and every entry of `remarkPlugins` and `rehypePlugins`, each with what replaces it. The other entries are yours to change.

::::plate[Named layouts](mark="=>", tone=expr)
0.x matched named layouts to folders. Templates aren't matched by folder, so for named layouts the notes include a `select_template` to paste into your config:

```js
select_template: (id) => (id.includes('/blog/') ? 'blog' : undefined),
```
::::

## `layout:` in frontmatter

::::plate[`layout:` is ordinary metadata](mark="layout", tone=text)
mdsvex doesn't read a `layout:` key any more, and there is no alias for it. The migrator renames it to `template:`. A document you haven't migrated keeps `layout:` as ordinary metadata, and gets the `default` template, or the one `select_template` picks:

```svx result templates
---
title: Hello
layout: blog
---

# Hello
```
::::

## Layout components

::::plate[A layout as a template](mark="<slot>", tone=comp)
A layout needs two changes to work as a template:

```svelte
<!-- before -->
<script>
	export let title;
</script>

<h1>{title}</h1>
<slot />
```

```svelte
<!-- after -->
<script>
	let { title, children } = $props();
</script>

<h1>{title}</h1>
{@render children()}
```

:::notes
`check_template` finds `<slot />`, `$$props`, `$$restProps` and `{...$$props}`. [`npx sv migrate svelte-5`][sv-migrate] migrates the rest of the component.

Element replacements stay in the module script, now `<script module>`, and keep working.
:::
::::

## Syntax highlighting in 0.x

0.x highlighted with Prism, through a `highlight.highlighter` that could be async and returned `{@html}`. Now twinkleplop highlights by default, and mdsvex escapes whatever a highlighter returns.

::::added
:::add[`highlight: false`](mark="false", tone=fence)
`highlight: false`
:::

:::add[`highlight: { alias: { x: 'y' } }`](mark="alias", tone=fence)
`highlight: { languages: { x: 'y' } }`
:::

:::add[`highlight: { highlighter(code, lang, meta, filename) }`, sync or async](mark="async", tone=fence)
`highlight: (code, { lang, meta, inline, filename }) => html`, sync only
:::

:::add[a highlighter returns `` {@html `…`} ``](mark="{@html", tone=expr)
a highlighter returns plain HTML
:::

:::add[`highlight.optimise`](mark="optim", tone=fence)
gone, highlighted code is always markup
:::

:::add[`escapeSvelte`](mark="escape", tone=expr)
gone, mdsvex escapes braces itself
:::

:::add[Prism and `prism-svelte`](mark="prism", tone=fence)
twinkleplop
:::

:::add[a Prism theme you add by hand](mark=".css", tone=fence)
a [twinkleplop theme](/docs/reference/highlighting#themes) you import
:::

:::add[a `pre` replacement doesn't apply to code blocks](mark="as pre", tone=comp)
it does, see [code block components](/docs/reference/highlighting#code-block-components)
:::
::::

`migrate_config` renames `alias` to `languages` and removes `optimise`. Alias targets are now twinkleplop's [languages](/docs/reference/highlighting#languages), so it flags Prism names twinkleplop doesn't have, such as `markup`. It can't rewrite a `highlighter`, so it leaves a `// TODO(mdsvex-migrate)` comment above it, and it flags every `escapeSvelte` import.

::::plate[A highlighter](mark="=>", tone=fence)
A highlighter changes in three ways. It takes an object as its second argument, it returns HTML without escaping it, and it can't be async. A Shiki highlighter goes from the first of these to the second:

```js
// before
import { escapeSvelte } from 'mdsvex';

const shiki = await createHighlighter({ themes: ['github-dark'], langs: ['ts'] });

const config = {
	highlight: {
		highlighter: async (code, lang = 'text') => {
			const html = escapeSvelte(shiki.codeToHtml(code, { lang, theme: 'github-dark' }));
			return `{@html \`${html}\` }`;
		}
	}
};
```

```js
// after
const shiki = await createHighlighter({ themes: ['github-dark'], langs: ['ts'] });

mdsvex({
	highlight: (code, { lang, inline }) =>
		inline ? null : shiki.codeToHtml(code, { lang: lang || 'text', theme: 'github-dark' })
});
```

:::notes
Or drop the highlighter and use the default. Either way, import a theme: highlighted code has no colours without one.
:::
::::

## Smart punctuation in 0.x

0.x curled quotes and made dashes and ellipses in every document unless you set `smartypants: false`. Now [`smart_punctuation`](/docs/reference/options#smart_punctuation) does that, and it is off unless you turn it on. `migrate_config` turns it on for a config that relied on the old default, so your pages keep their typography:

::::added
:::add[no `smartypants` option](mark="{}", tone=text)
`smart_punctuation: true` is added
:::

:::add[`smartypants: true`](mark="true", tone=text)
`smart_punctuation: true`
:::

:::add[`smartypants: false`](mark="false", tone=text)
removed, off is the default
:::

:::add[`smartypants: { quotes, ellipses }`](mark="quotes", tone=text)
`smart_punctuation: { quotes, ellipses }`
:::

:::add[`dashes: true`, the default: `--` is an em dash, `---` is left alone](mark="--", tone=text)
`--` is an en dash and `---` an em dash
:::

:::add[`dashes: 'oldschool'`](mark="---", tone=text)
`dashes: true`, which now means the same
:::

:::add[`dashes: 'inverted'`](mark="-- ---", tone=text)
`dashes: true`, and the documents are rewritten
:::

:::add[`backticks`, which curled <code>&#96;&#96;quoted''</code>](mark="``''", tone=text)
gone, the documents are rewritten
:::
::::

::::plate[Dashes](mark="--", tone=text)
The dashes are the one change that shows. With the 0.x default, `--` was an em dash. It is an en dash now, as it is in pandoc, cmark and LaTeX. So that pages render what they did, pass your old `smartypants` value to `migrate` when it converts your documents, `true` if you never set it, as the script under [run the migrator](/docs/guides/migrating-from-0x#run-the-migrator) does:

```js
migrate(source, { smartypants: true });
```

:::notes
It turns each `--` in text into `---`, keeps a `---` that 0.x left alone as `\---`, swaps the two for `'inverted'`, and turns <code>&#96;&#96;quoted''</code> into `"quoted"`. Code, urls and attributes are not touched. The notes from `migrate_config` say which of this applies to your config.
:::

:::edges
- Three things 0.x changed are left alone now, because they are not prose: a string inside a Svelte expression, an attribute such as `--css-var="x"` on a component, and the text of an autolinked url.
- And `--name` after a space is read as a command line flag, where 0.x gave it a dash.
:::
::::
